FORMAT: 1A
title: Search
description: Documentation for the search query language, pagination, and errors shared by every searchable SparkPost API resource.
full: true

# Search

A search is a `QUERY` request to a resource's own collection URL, such as `/api/v1/templates`, with a JSON body.
`QUERY` is an HTTP method for a safe, idempotent read that carries a request body, like a `GET` with a body.
`GET` on the same URL keeps its existing meaning, so a search and a list are two separate operations.

These resources support search:

| Resource  | Action                                                      | Fields                                  |
|-----------|-------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|
| Templates | [Search Templates](/api/templates/#templates-query-search-templates) | [Templates fields](#header-templates-fields) |

Every searchable resource shares the request body, the query language, pagination, the response envelope, and the errors on this page.
Only the fields differ from one resource to another.

With cURL, set the method with `-X QUERY`:

```
curl -X QUERY \
-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
-H "Authorization: 14ac5499cfdd2bb2859e4476d2e5b1d2bad079bf" \
-d '{"query": "welcome status:published"}' \
"https://api.sparkpost.com/api/v1/templates"
```

## Request body

The body is one JSON object, sent with `Content-Type: application/json`.

| Field      | Type             | Default                                                  | Description |
|------------|------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|-------------|
| `query`    | string           | Matches every item your API key can see                  | A query in the [query language](#header-query-language). |
| `sort`     | array of objects | `[{ "field": "last_update_time", "direction": "desc" }]` | Exactly one object, with a `field` and a `direction` of `asc` or `desc`. The resource's field table lists the fields you can sort by. |
| `per_page` | number           | `25`                                                     | The number of results on a page, a whole number from 1 to 1,000. |
| `cursor`   | string           | `initial`                                                | The `next_cursor` value from the previous page. Leave it out, or send `initial`, for the first page. |
| `include`  | array of strings | Adds nothing                                             | Fields to add to the page. The only value is `total_count`, which adds the number of items the query matches. |

A field sent as JSON `null` takes its default, and so does an empty `sort` array.
Unknown fields are ignored.
A body that sends the same key twice in one object is a 400, and so is a `sort` array with two or more objects or an `include` value other than `total_count`.
The body is limited to 64 KB, and a larger body is a 413.

## Query language

A query is one or more terms, separated by whitespace.
A term is a **qualifier**, such as `status:published`, which restricts one field, or **bare text**, such as `welcome`, which has no field in front of it.
You can join terms with `AND`, `OR`, `NOT`, and parentheses.
An empty query, or one that holds only whitespace, matches every item your API key can see.

These queries all search Templates:

| Query                                                        | Finds templates that |
|--------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|
| `welcome`                                                    | have `welcome` in the name, ID, subaccount ID, or description |
| `welcome status:published`                                   | have `welcome` in one of those fields and are published |
| `v2-fr subaccount_id:5`                                      | have `v2-fr` in one of those fields and belong to subaccount 5 |
| `name:welc`                                                  | have `welc` anywhere in the name |
| `id:=welcome`                                                | have the ID `welcome`, and not `welcome-v2` |
| `name:"welcome email"`                                       | have `welcome email`, with the space, anywhere in the name |
| `(name:welcome OR name:onboarding) status:draft,published`   | have `welcome` or `onboarding` in the name, and are drafts or published |
| `-status:draft last_update_time:>=2026-01-01`                | are not drafts and were last updated on or after 1 January 2026 |
| `last_update_time:2026-01-01..2026-06-30`                    | were last updated between 1 January and 30 June 2026, both days included |

### Two operators

A qualifier is a field name, an operator, and a value, with no whitespace between them.
Every operator starts with a colon:

* `:=` matches the whole value, on every field type.
* `:` matches as much as the field's type allows, which the table shows for each type.
* `:>`, `:>=`, `:<`, `:<=`, and `:a..b` compare, on `number` and `date` fields.

| Field type | `:` matches                      | `:=` matches       | `:>`, `:>=`, `:<`, `:<=`, and `..` |
|------------|----------------------------------|--------------------|------------------------------------|
| `string`   | any part of the value            | the whole value    | 400                                |
| `keyword`  | the whole value                  | the whole value    | 400                                |
| `enum`     | one value from a fixed set       | the same as `:`    | 400                                |
| `boolean`  | `true` or `false`                | the same as `:`    | 400                                |
| `number`   | the value                        | the same as `:`    | yes                                |
| `date`     | the day or the instant the value names | the same as `:` | yes, against the edges of that day or instant |

Every match is case-insensitive, `:=` included, so `name:WELC` and `status:Draft` work.
A `:` match on a `string` field finds a part of any length, so `name:w` finds every template with a `w` in its name.
An operator that a field's type does not accept is a 400 that names the operators it does accept.
For example, `name:>x` is a 400.

A `=` with no colon in front of it is ordinary text, so `id=welcome` is bare text that searches for `id=welcome`.
A field name and `=` directly before a quote is a 400 that suggests `:=`, so `name="welcome email"` is a 400 at the `=`.

A field's type is fixed.
The field tables at the end of this page list each field with its type.

### Bare text

Bare text is a word, or a quoted value, with no field in front of it.
It searches each of the resource's Default fields with `:`, and matches when any one of them matches.
For Templates, the Default fields are `name`, `id`, `subaccount_id`, and `description`, so `welc` searches the same fields as `(name:welc OR id:welc OR subaccount_id:welc OR description:welc)`.
When your API key cannot query a Default field, bare text skips that field instead of failing.

Two words separated by whitespace are two terms, and a match needs both.
So `welcome email` finds `Email for welcome series`, with the words in any order and in any fields.
Quote the words to search for them together: `"welcome email"` finds the two words next to each other and in that order.
The quoted value is a match on any part of a field, not on whole words, so `"welcome email"` also finds `prewelcome emails`.

A lowercase `or` is text like any other word.
So `welcome or onboarding` is three terms that a match needs all of, and one of them searches for `or`.

### Quotes only delimit a value

Quotes mark where a value starts and ends, so the value can hold whitespace or a special character.
Quotes do not change how a value matches:

* `name:"welcome email"` is a partial match on a value that holds a space. It is not a phrase match on whole words, so it finds `Big welcome emails`.
* Quoting never makes a match case-sensitive. `name:"Welcome Email"` and `name:"welcome email"` find the same templates.

To match a whole value, use `:=`: `name:="welcome email"` finds only the template named `welcome email`, in any case.

### Escape special characters

These characters have a special meaning outside quotes: `"`, `\`, `(`, `)`, and `:`.
To search for text that holds one of them, put the text in double quotes: `name:"Re: welcome"`.

Inside a qualifier value, these characters also have a special meaning: `,`, `<`, `>`, `*`, `?`, `/`, and the sequence `..`.
Quote a value that holds one of them: `name:"a/b test"`.
In bare text they are ordinary characters, so `welcome,email` is one search for the text `welcome,email`.

Every other character is ordinary text, so `Don't miss out`, `[Draft] Welcome`, and `2+2=4` need no quotes.
A `~` directly after the colon of a qualifier is a 400.

Inside quotes, `\"` is a double quote and `\\` is a backslash.
Any other backslash sequence is a 400, and so is an empty pair of quotes, except after `:=`.

A quoted value can hold a tab.
It cannot hold a carriage return or a line feed, so no query matches across a line break.
To find a description that holds a line break, search for text on one side of it.

Whitespace is a space, a tab, a carriage return, or a line feed.
Any other space character is a 400 wherever it appears, which includes the non-breaking space that text copied from a web page can carry.
Invisible characters are a 400 too: control characters, the zero-width and invisible formatting characters U+00AD, U+200B, U+2060, and U+FEFF, and the bidirectional control characters.
U+200D and U+FE0F are ordinary characters, so an emoji sequence that uses them is one word.

Whitespace is not allowed around `:`, `:=`, `,`, `..`, or a comparison operator, so `name: welc` is a 400.

### Lists

A `,` between values is an `OR` of those values on one field: `status:draft,published` matches either status.
A list holds at most 50 values, and a value must follow each `,` directly.
So `status:draft, published` and `status:draft ,published` are both a 400.
A list works on `string`, `keyword`, and `enum` fields only.
On any other type it is a 400, so `published:true,false` is a 400.

Bare text cannot end with `,`, so `welcome, onboarding` is a 400 at the comma.

### Null and empty values

`field:null` matches items where the field has no value, and `-field:null` matches items where it has one.
Every Templates field always has a value.
`field:=null` is the same as `field:null`.
`null` is not case-sensitive.
To search for the text `null`, quote it: `name:"null"`.
A `null` inside a list, a range, or a comparison is a 400.

An empty string is a value, and `field:=""` finds it.
For example, `description:=""` finds templates with an empty description.
`""` works only after `:=`, only as the whole value, and only on a `string` field.
So `name:""`, `id:=abc,""`, and `""` on any other field type are each a 400.

### Combine terms

Whitespace between two terms means `AND`, so `welcome status:published` and `welcome AND status:published` are the same query.
`OR` matches when either term matches.
`NOT`, or `-` directly before a term, negates the term.
Parentheses group terms, up to five levels deep.

`OR` binds more tightly than `AND`, so `welcome onboarding OR signup` means `welcome AND (onboarding OR signup)`.
Add parentheses to make the grouping clear:

* `(name:welcome OR name:onboarding) status:published`
* `welcome (status:draft OR has_draft:true)`
* `NOT (status:draft OR status:published_with_draft)`

The keywords `AND`, `OR`, and `NOT` are uppercase.
Lowercase `and`, `or`, and `not` are text.
A keyword is a keyword everywhere outside quotes, including after `:` or `:=`, so `name:AND` is a 400.
To search for the word, quote it: `name:"AND"`.
A keyword needs whitespace after it: `NOT (status:draft OR has_draft:true)` works, and `NOT(status:draft)` is a 400.
Two terms need whitespace between them too, so `(welcome)(email)` and `"welcome"email` are each a 400.
A query cannot end with a keyword, so `welcome AND` is a 400.

### Negation

`-` and `NOT` negate a qualifier, bare text, or a group in parentheses.
Each term takes one negation:

* `-status:draft OR name:welc` means `(NOT status:draft) OR name:welc`.
* `-(status:draft OR status:published)` negates the group.
* `--status:draft`, `NOT -status:draft`, `-NOT status:draft`, and `- status:draft` are each a 400.

Bare text cannot start with `-`, so search for the text `-draft` as `"-draft"`.
A qualifier value can start with `-`, so `id:-draft` finds a template whose ID contains `-draft`.

### Comparisons and ranges

`number` and `date` fields accept comparisons and ranges.
On any other type, they are a 400.

* `:>`, `:>=`, `:<`, and `:<=` compare with one value: `last_update_time:>=2026-01-01`.
* `:a..b` is a range that includes both edges: `last_update_time:2026-01-01..2026-06-30`.

A range needs both edges.
For a range with one edge, use `:>=` or `:<=`.
A range whose first edge is after its second edge is a 400 rather than an empty page.
A value that holds `..` must be quoted.
Outside a qualifier, `..` is ordinary text, so a bare `2026-01-01..2026-06-30` is one word of bare text.

### Dates

A date is `YYYY-MM-DD`, such as `2026-01-01`, or a date and a time, `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm`, with optional seconds and optional milliseconds, such as `"2026-01-01T09:30:00.000"`.
A date and time holds colons, so quote it.
A date and time can end with `Z` or an offset from `-12:00` to `+14:00`, such as `"2026-01-01T09:30-05:00"`.

A date or time with no offset is UTC.
A date with no time is the whole of that UTC day, so `last_update_time:2026-01-01` matches any time on 1 January 2026, UTC.
`:>` starts after the day or instant that the value names, and `:<` ends before it.

Other ISO 8601 forms, such as `2026`, `2026-W38`, and `20260101`, are a 400.
An offset on a date with no time, such as `2026-01-01Z`, is a 400.
A date that does not exist, such as `2026-02-30`, or a time that does not exist, such as `"2026-01-01T25:61"`, is a 400.

### Numbers

A number is digits, with an optional leading `-` and an optional decimal part: `-12`, `0.5`, `007`.
Other forms, such as `1e3` and `0x10`, are a 400.
So is a number that search cannot store exactly: an integer larger than 2<sup>53</sup>, or a decimal more precise than a 64-bit floating-point number.
No Templates field is a `number` field.

### Field names and values

Field names are case-sensitive, and are written as the field table prints them.
An unknown field is a 400 that names the nearest field, so `nmae:welc` is a 400 instead of a text search.

`enum` and `boolean` fields accept only the values the field table lists.
An unknown value is a 400, so `status:archived`, `published:maybe`, and `published:1` are each a 400.
Values are not case-sensitive, so `published:TRUE` works.

### Grammar

This is the query grammar in EBNF, using the RFC 5234 ABNF core rules (`ALPHA`, `DIGIT`, `SP`, `HTAB`, `LF`, `CR`, `DQUOTE`) and RFC 7405 `%s` for case-sensitive terminals.
`{ }` is repetition, `1*` is one or more, `[ ]` is optional, and `|` is alternation.
A terminal with the `%s` prefix is case-sensitive.
A plain quoted terminal is not, which is why `null` is not case-sensitive and `AND` is.
`except` subtracts from the production on its left, comparing the whole token the production matched and never a prefix, so `ANDROID`, `ORDER`, and `nullify` are ordinary words.
Scope is every item your API key can see.

The syntax runs over tokens:

```
query      = [ ws ] [ expression ] [ ws ]          ; empty or blank matches everything in Scope
expression = clause { ws [ %s"AND" ws ] clause }   ; AND: every clause must match
clause     = term { ws %s"OR" ws term }            ; OR: any term may match; binds tighter
term       = [ modifier ] ( group | qualifier | text )
modifier   = "-" | %s"NOT" ws
group      = "(" [ ws ] expression [ ws ] ")"
qualifier  = field ( match | equals | compare )
match      = ":"  ( null | range | list )
equals     = ":=" ( null | empty | list )
compare    = ( ":>=" | ":<=" | ":>" | ":<" ) value
list       = value { "," value }                   ; 50 values maximum
range      = value ".." value
text       = quoted | word
```

The tokens follow.
The longest match wins within a token, and a token ends at whitespace or at a structural character.
`..` ends a token inside a qualifier value only.

```
word       = ( word-initial [ { word-char } word-final ] ) except keyword
word-final = word-char except ","
value      = quoted | ( 1*value-char ) except ( keyword | null )
field      = ( ALPHA { ALPHA | DIGIT | "_" } ) except keyword
quoted     = DQUOTE 1*( qchar | "\" DQUOTE | "\" "\" ) DQUOTE
empty      = DQUOTE DQUOTE
null       = "null"                              ; case-insensitive
keyword    = %s"AND" | %s"OR" | %s"NOT"
ws         = 1*( SP | HTAB | LF | CR )

word-initial = word-char except "-", "." and ","
word-char    = any Unicode scalar except ws, uspace, control and structural
value-char   = word-char except value-structural, and except a "." followed by a "."
qchar        = any Unicode scalar except DQUOTE, "\", uspace, and control other than HTAB
uspace       = any Unicode scalar in general category Zs, Zl or Zp, except SP
control      = any Unicode scalar in general category Cc, plus the zero-width and
               invisible formatting characters U+00AD, U+200B, U+2060 and U+FEFF, plus
               every Bidi_Control character: U+061C, U+200E, U+200F, U+202A-U+202E
               and U+2066-U+2069. U+200D and U+FE0F stay literal, so a joined emoji
               sequence is one word
structural       = DQUOTE | "\" | "(" | ")" | ":"
value-structural = "," | "<" | ">" | "*" | "?" | "/"
```

## Limits

| Limit                                  | Value |
|----------------------------------------|-------|
| Length of a query                      | 4,096 bytes, in UTF-8 |
| Terms in a query                       | 10    |
| Levels of nested parentheses           | 5     |
| Values in a list                       | 50    |
| Values in a query                      | 100   |
| Length of one value                    | 1,024 bytes, in UTF-8 |
| Results on a page                      | 1,000 |
| Request body                           | 64 KB |

A term is a qualifier or bare text, counted before bare text expands to the Default fields.
A list counts as one term.
The nesting limit counts depth, not the number of parentheses, so `(welcome) (email) (promo) (sale) (summer) (winter)` has a depth of one, and `((((((welcome))))))` has a depth of six and is a 400.

The values in a query count every value in every term.
Bare text counts once for each Default field it searches, which is four times on Templates.

The value length is measured after escapes are resolved and after the query is normalized to Unicode NFC.
The same visible text is therefore measured the same way however it was typed.

## Consistency

Search results are eventually consistent.
A change to an item typically appears in search within a few seconds of the write.
To read an item as you last wrote it, retrieve it by its ID, such as with [Retrieve a Template](/api/templates/#templates-get-retrieve-a-template), `GET /api/v1/templates/{id}`.

## Pagination

Search returns one page at a time.
To get the next page, send the same `query` and `sort` again, with `cursor` set to the `next_cursor` value of the page you have.
A response with no `next_cursor` is the last page.

A cursor is opaque.
It works only with the `query` and the `sort` that produced it, and only for the same set of items the request can see, which the API key and the `X-MSYS-SUBACCOUNT` header decide.
A cursor sent with any of them changed is a 400 with the code `invalid_cursor`.
`include` and `per_page` can change from one page to the next.

There is no previous-page cursor.
To go back, keep the cursors of the pages you have read.
To start from the last result, reverse the `direction` of `sort`.

## Response envelope

A search returns `results`, an array of items, and `next_cursor`, the cursor for the next page.
With `"include": ["total_count"]`, it also returns `total_count`, the number of items the query matches.
`total_count` counts up to 10,000, so a query that matches more items returns `10000`.
Without `include`, the response has no `total_count`.
The fields inside each item belong to the resource.

```
{
  "results": [
    { "id": "welcome-email", "name": "Welcome Email" }
  ],
  "next_cursor": "eyJhIjpbMTc4NDcyNTUwMDAwMCwiNSIsInN1YjUtd2VsY29tZSJdLCJmIjoiMklLalFEOGlxaW8yYklzWGdzdEFhQSJ9",
  "total_count": 3
}
```

## Errors

An error returns an `errors` array in the [standard format](/api/#header-errors).
A query error carries `position`, the 0-based offset in the query where the problem starts.

```
{
  "errors": [
    {
      "message": "status is one of draft, published, published_with_draft",
      "code": "invalid_search_query",
      "position": 15
    }
  ]
}
```

| Status | Code                   | Cause |
|--------|------------------------|-------|
| 400    | `invalid_search_query` | The query does not follow the query language, exceeds a limit, or uses a field the API key cannot query. The error carries a `position`. |
| 400    | `invalid_param`        | The body is not a JSON object, repeats a key, or has a field outside its bounds, such as two `sort` objects or a `per_page` of 0. |
| 400    | `invalid_cursor`       | The cursor was issued for a different query, sort, or set of items the request can see. |
| 401    |                        | The API key is missing or not valid. |
| 405    |                        | The URL does not accept the method. |
| 413    |                        | The body is larger than 64 KB. |
| 429    |                        | Too many requests. See [Rate Limiting](/api/#header-rate-limiting). |
| 503    |                        | Search is not available, or the search timed out. The response carries a `Retry-After` header with the number of seconds to wait. A search never returns a partial page. |

A query that search refuses as too complex to run is a 400 `invalid_search_query` with no `position`, because no one place in the query causes it.
The limits keep a query from reaching that point.

### Error positions

`position` counts Unicode scalar values, which are code points, not bytes and not UTF-16 code units, in the query as you sent it.
The position is where the token that caused the error starts:

| Query                 | Position | Why |
|-----------------------|----------|-----|
| `name:"welc`          | 5        | The closing quote is missing, and the opening quote is at 5. |
| `(a OR b`             | 0        | The parenthesis at 0 is not closed. |
| `status:nope`         | 7        | `nope` is not a status, and the value starts at 7. |
| `name:"a\tb"`         | 7        | `\t` is not an escape, and the backslash is at 7. |
| `na-me:welc`          | 5        | `na-me` is bare text, and a `:` cannot follow bare text. |
| `name:AND`            | 5        | `AND` is a keyword, and a keyword cannot be a value. |
| `welcome AND`         | 8        | Nothing follows the `AND` at 8. |
| `- welcome`           | 0        | Whitespace follows the `-` at 0. |

A query error that exceeds a limit names the first item past the limit: the 11th term, the 51st value in a list, or the sixth `(`.

The query is checked for syntax first, then for limits, then for field types and API key access.
So the first syntax error is reported even when the query also has a later error.
For example, `(status:nope` is a 400 at 0, for the parenthesis that is not closed, and not at 8 for the unknown status.

## Templates fields

Search on Templates with [Search Templates](/api/templates/#templates-query-search-templates).

| Field | Type | `:` matches | Comparisons | `,` list | Default field | Sortable |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | `string` | any part of the value | no | yes | yes | no |
| `id` | `string` | any part of the value | no | yes | yes | no |
| `description` | `string` | any part of the value | no | yes | yes | no |
| `subaccount_id`, alias `subaccount` | `keyword` | the whole value, primary account without `X-MSYS-SUBACCOUNT` only | no | yes | yes | no |
| `status` | `enum` | `draft`, `published`, or `published_with_draft` | no | yes | no | no |
| `published` | `boolean` | `true` or `false` | no | no | no | no |
| `has_published` | `boolean` | `true` or `false` | no | no | no | no |
| `has_draft` | `boolean` | `true` or `false` | no | no | no | no |
| `shared_with_subaccounts` | `boolean` | `true` or `false` | no | no | no | no |
| `last_update_time`, alias `last_updated` | `date` | the day or the instant | yes | no | no | yes |
| `created_at` | `date` | the day or the instant | yes | no | no | yes |

The Default fields are `name`, `id`, `subaccount_id`, and `description`.
Bare text searches these four fields.

An alias works the same way as the field it names, in every respect.
So `subaccount:5`, `subaccount:=5`, and `last_updated:>2025-10-10` all work.

The API key and the `X-MSYS-SUBACCOUNT` header decide which templates a search can see, as they do for [List all Templates](/api/templates/#templates-get-list-all-templates):

| API key         | `X-MSYS-SUBACCOUNT` | Templates searched |
|-----------------|---------------------|--------------------|
| primary account | not set             | the primary account's templates and every subaccount's templates |
| primary account | `0`                 | the primary account's templates only |
| primary account | a subaccount ID     | the subaccount's templates, and the primary account's templates shared with subaccounts |
| subaccount      | not set             | the subaccount's templates, and the primary account's templates shared with subaccounts |

A query can narrow this set. It cannot widen it.

A `subaccount_id` qualifier works only with a primary account API key sent without the `X-MSYS-SUBACCOUNT` header.
Otherwise it is a 400, and bare text skips `subaccount_id`.
With the `X-MSYS-SUBACCOUNT` header set to a subaccount ID, `shared_with_subaccounts:false` returns the same templates as the `shared_with_subaccounts=false` parameter of [List all Templates](/api/templates/#templates-get-list-all-templates).

Sort by `last_update_time` or `created_at`.
To narrow by name or status instead, filter on it: `status:draft` with the default sort finds the newest drafts.
Items with the same value in the `sort` field are ordered by subaccount ID and then by template ID, both ascending, so the order is always the same.
